                <pb facs="0421" n="297" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg297"/>

                <head>No. 176.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Angell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-08-30"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 30,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 123.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 173, of the 18th of June
                    last, respecting the immunity granted to Protestant Chinese converts, has been
                    received and read with much interest.</p>
                <p>The Christian world cannot but be deeply impressed by this action of the Chinese
                    Government, which manifests so liberal and just a disposition toward such of its
                    subjects as are led to adopt the Christian faith.</p>
                <p>A copy of your dispatch has been communicated for the information of the American
                    Board of Commissioners for Foreign Missions at Boston.</p>
                <p>I am, sir,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
